# Every edge of every graph the filings make, one row per (graph, filing, from, to).
#
# One relation, not four spellings
#   The relations that measure these graphs, the law that compares their scopes, and the drawing all
#   need the same union over `composition/parts.sqlc` and `units/conversions.sqlc`. Written out by
#   hand in each, four spellings of one relation are four chances to mistype one, and no law here
#   could compare them: an expression written inline has no name for a law to count.
#
# The node is an opaque id
#   That is the one compromise in this file. A layer graph's node is `(filing, layer)` and a unit
#   graph's node is `(unit)`, keys of different widths, and one relation carrying both must pick a
#   common type. Everything downstream of this relation counts (nodes, edges, components, the two
#   figures), and a count needs only that distinct nodes have distinct ids. That is why joining the
#   key into one text is right here and would be a defect in the drawing: drawing needs to join a
#   node back to the model, and a joined text cannot be joined to anything.
#   `rank/graph_nodes.sqlc` attaches each graph's node facts at their own grain and joins the key
#   into text afterwards, the same idiom in the order that works.
#
# The separator is `/`, and nothing forbids one in a layer name
#   `pm:Layer/name` is an `xs:token`, so two distinct layers could become one node, and both the
#   drawing and the measurement would collapse the same way and agree. On this corpus no two layers
#   collide, by luck rather than by construction, and this is the one place that has to be true
#   rather than four.
#
# `filing` is the per-filing cut, not part of the edge's identity
#   `rank/decomposition.sqlc` asks whether cutting each graph by filing loses a loop, so it needs
#   the column; the whole-corpus count ignores it. A caller that wants the corpus-wide edges takes
#   `DISTINCT graph, from_node, to_node` and drops it.
#
# Probe
#   Count the distinct `(filing, layer)` pairs and the distinct joined strings; they must be equal,
#   and a layer name containing `/` is what would make them differ.
-- composition/parts.sqlc and units/conversions.sqlc, each labelled with the graph it is an edge of.
SELECT 'layers' AS graph,
       p.composition                            AS filing,
       p.composition  || '/' || p.composed_layer AS from_node,
       p.part_filing  || '/' || p.part_layer     AS to_node
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
UNION ALL
SELECT 'units', c.filing, c.from_unit, c.to_unit
FROM (
    :compose(units/conversions.sqlc)
) c
